A||||16||SilverFieroV6||my car cought on fire today||||8||
Z||000000||SilverFieroV6||07-21-2001||10:45 PM||||let me first say the car is ok, and was saved with minimal dammage.<BR>  <P>Last week right before I went to the kick hill swap meet I had the rear calipers, rotors, and pads replaced on my gold 86 se.  On the way back to NJ from CT I noticed a burning smell coming from the rear tire.  I brought it back to my mechanic and told him what was going on and told him if it was the e-brake  and it was getting stuck to just disconnect it as I dont use it.  Got it back the other day and didnt drive it agian till today.  I was on my way to work (only like a 20 minute ride), about half way there, I noticed that burning smell from the rear tire.  I pulled in a office complex and looked at the rear tire.  It was smoking real bad, so I jacked the car up and started to loosen the lug nuts when I look throught the rim at the rotor and see small flames.  I hear this loud pop and white smoke pours out from around the tire.  The smoke clears and I start to loosen the lug nuts agian and notice there are no more flames.  When I got the tire off I noticed that the brake line  right where it connects to the caliper had come off( im guessing from the heat and what not).  I think that the brake line came off (which was the loud pop) and all the white smoke was the brake fluid putting out the fire.  Im not exactly sure  but thats what I think.  I had the car towed to  my mechanic, he has some new guy working for him and he is who worked on my car.  The new guy is now fired.  I need new rotors, calipers, bearings, pads, and dont know what else yet.<BR>Sam<P>------------------<BR>Gold 86 SE V6 4sp daily driver<BR>Silver 85 SE V6 auto ( actually running now)<p>[This message has been edited by SilverFieroV6 (edited 07-21-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||CoolBlue87GT||07-21-2001||11:05 PM||||Oh boy, glad it wasn't a total wreck.<P>That would have scared the heck out of me.<P>||||reg||6
Z||000002||Adrift||07-21-2001||11:09 PM||||Did you have the recalls performed are your cars? Fiero's do tend to catch on Fire...<P>"let me first say the car is ok," - I don't know if I'm only speaking for myself, but my first concern would be your own saftey, not the saftey of your car!<p>[This message has been edited by Adrift (edited 07-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||DecadenceR||07-21-2001||11:37 PM||||Damn... That really sucks. Good thing that everything's okay with it though. WEll, as okay as it can be.<P>Where is your mechanic? I need a decent one, i do a lot of stuff myself, but the only place around me is pep-boys, and I don't go there unless I'm dying, or Sav-On but they're way expensive and only have one bay so they're hard to get a car into.||||reg||1
Z||000004||SilverFieroV6||07-21-2001||11:47 PM||||he is on rt 37 in toms river although I know he really doesnt like working on fiero's  the only way I get him to do it is he is a friend of the family<BR>Sam<P>Yeah I am ok, just a little shook up sitting there looking at flames on my car was the worst feeling I ever had.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTBro||07-22-2001||12:45 AM||||That same thing happened to me last month.  It was not fun.  I was 40 miles away from home and my right rear caliper froze and the pads caught on fire.  The brake line melted off, but that didn't put out the fire.  I was able to find a water hose and bucket nearby to put out the fire.<P>I ended up bringing it back home on a flatbed trailer.  The damage was about $550 in parts.  I did all the work myself.<P>I replaced both rear calipers and rotors and brake pads.  It was a royal pain in the butt to find the calipers I needed around here.  I also replaced the brake lines with Earl's steel braided lines.  I didn't replace the bearing hubs.  The one that endured the heat didn't seem to have any damage.  Plus they would have put me $200 more into the hole.<P>Last week I went ahead and replaced the rotors and pads on the front brakes as well.  So now I have new brakes all the way around.  Stops good now... no squeaking or squealing or smoke or flames.  It cost me a trip to my first fiero show in San Antonio (Fiero Roundup 5), but at least I can drive my car now!||||reg||1
Z||000006||IndySid||07-22-2001||01:31 AM||||I had a similar incident happen to me with my car and it scared the CR*P out of me.  All I could think of was all the work, effort and money I put into the damn thing.  I have now installed a portable fire extinguisher in my trunk just in case it happens again.  I'd rather be safe than sorry.||||reg||1
Z||000007||88CoupeV6||07-22-2001||04:12 PM||||Wow, I had the right back on my yellow 88GT smoking on the way home from Kick Hill too. We stopped for gas and Jaygee was driving it. When we started back up she must have not released the ebrake all the way and it started smoking. We pulled over and I Jacked up the wheel. I could spin it fine after releasing the e-brake.<P>I am glad that you didn't have a lot of damage or a huge fire  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh <B>(known by TheFiero64 for first 1239 posts)</B><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
Z||000008||SilverFieroV6||07-25-2001||12:59 AM||||Well the car is fixed.  I am picking it up tommorow.  new pads, rotor, caliper, and brake line for that side.   I'm just glad that the car is ok and i will have it back tommorow.<BR>Sam<P>------------------<BR>Gold 86 SE V6 4sp daily driver<BR>Silver 85 SE V6 auto ( actually running now)||||reg||1
Z||000009||stsmithgt||07-25-2001||09:50 AM||||I just <I>love</I> it when someone gets to work on my car that doesn't know what there doing. Don't you? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
